Information Causality and Renormalisation

A technical note on why IC-violating resources obstruct conventional local RG flows.

Background

Renormalisation group (RG) flows describe how a theory changes under coarse-graining: microscopic degrees of freedom are grouped into blocks and mapped to effective variables at a larger length scale. In most familiar settings, RG is useful because it comes with three expectations:

Locality (of the RG step): the coarse-graining map can be implemented by local operations with bounded causal radius (e.g., a constant-depth circuit of finite-range gates).

Finite-parameter closure: after each step, the effective theory remains describable by finitely many local (or rapidly decaying) couplings whose number does not grow with system size or RG depth.

Operational faithfulness: the coarse theory reproduces the operational predictions of the microscopic theory for the class of protocols the theory allows.

Information causality (IC) is an information-theoretic principle: if Alice sends Bob 
𝑚
m classical bits, Bob should not gain access to more than 
𝑚
m bits of useful information about Alice’s remote data (in the precise sense of the IC inequality). Quantum theory respects IC: entanglement does not enable a small classical message to unlock arbitrarily large addressable remote information.

A hypothetical non-signalling resource such as a Popescu–Rohrlich (PR) box violates IC. In particular, PR correlations allow perfect random-access coding: with only one classical bit from Alice to Bob, Bob can recover any chosen one of 
𝑁
