Remarks

The argument does not assume spectral gaps, equilibrium, or entanglement area laws. It relies only on causal locality (finite-depth / Lieb–Robinson type constraints) and finite local information capacity.

Stating the contradiction in terms of random-access operational capability (rather than raw mutual information) avoids overclaiming. The fire-hose effect is about addressable access, not bulk transmission.

CPTP alone does not constrain information flow; the key constraint is localizability with bounded depth, which yields a causal radius and the shell-encoding bound.

The result applies to classical, quantum, and GPT settings that admit an IC-violating non-signalling resource as part of their operational content.

Conclusion

IC-violating resources enable a fire-hose effect: a constant-size classical interface can unlock an unbounded family of addressable remote bits. Conventional RG depends on the opposite intuition: coarse-graining should yield a local, finite-parameter effective description whose operational content can be mediated through bounded-radius locality. Under operational faithfulness, these requirements collide. Therefore, information causality emerges as a consistency condition for the existence of a local, finite-parameter RG description: violating IC forces one to abandon locality of the RG step, finite-parameter closure, or faithfulness.
